breaks.fix:
Judge evidence on the pre-block state alone. A proof is valid when:
- both halves name the same name and sit in the same slot;
- the halves differ;
- both verify under that name's current key, which the current-key rule already pins.

Drop the membership and power lookup. So that no honest non-member ever signs a pair, the driver signs votes and proposals only when its own name is in that height's set. Today every node runs a driver (node/node.go:607), and castVote signs whatever the membership (consensus/driver.go:314-338). This gives a key holder nothing new, since the free self-freeze already lets it freeze its name. No record and no stored keys are needed.

required_changes.change:
Judge evidence only from the state before the block and the two signed messages, and drop the lookup of the validator set 'recorded for its height'.

A proof is valid when all of these hold:
- its two votes share height, round and type, or its two proposals share height and round;
- both carry the same signer name, byte for byte as registered;
- they differ in the block they sign;
- both verify under that name's current key in the pre-block state;
- the name is not already frozen.

There is no membership or power lookup. By default there is also no age limit (owner question 3), so RetainBlocks bounds only the record; rewrite plan lines 704-709 and 717-718 to match. In place of the membership check, a node's driver signs votes and proposals only at heights where its own name is in the set. Settle 'one spelling per name' (line 764) before the freeze, as E34 does for keys.

Limit what evidence may freeze, using power already in state. A block's evidence freezes a name only if, afterwards, all frozen names (whatever froze them) hold under a third of the power of seated and frozen names together. Power here is a name's static power, or the stake delegated to it.

Evidence over this cap stays valid while its key is current, and it waits. Alternatively the ⅔ re-keys the name, which makes the evidence moot. Refuse new delegations to a frozen name, so the frozen total cannot be inflated.

State the scope in the plan and in docs/security.md:
- the freeze handles double-signing by under a third of the power;
- persistent double-signing or key loss at a third or more is recovered by a re-genesis, with new keys and a new network id (E20);
- losing the largest validator must still leave more than ⅔ of the rest online.

Keep evidence by whether it is still valid, not by set membership. A node keeps a pending proof until one of these happens:
- a committed block carries it;
- the name is frozen;
- the convicted key is no longer the name's current key.

Remove the membership filter in slashEvidence (node/node.go:519-523) and the pruning in commit (node/node.go:889-894), and fix recordLateDoubleSign's comment (:497-500). Optionally, save pending proofs in the snapshot, since today they live only in memory (node/node.go:121).

Check a pending self-service key or bank change again when it falls due, not only when it is filed. The change lapses if:
- its name is in the set that governs that block; or
- its new key has meanwhile become some name's key.

The ⅔ name action's NewKey must also not be any other name's key.

Within a block:
1. judge every item against the pre-block state;
2. apply freezes (from evidence, holders or the ⅔);
3. apply the ⅔ name action;
4. apply the changes that fall due.

A freeze then cancels a change maturing in the same block, and the frozen list keeps the convicted key.

rejected.reason:
Each adds permanent ledger state. These three changes close the same cases without any:
- the fifth change (re-check a pending change when it falls due);
- the first change (the driver signs only while its name is seated);
- the third change (every unfreeze needs a new key).

Saving pending proofs in the snapshot is optional, not required.

owner_questions:
Holder self-freezes: may the ⅔ lift a holder's false-alarm self-freeze and let it keep its key? Recommended no: every unfreeze re-keys, as you put it ('an unfreeze will pick a new key'), with no extra state. If yes, three things follow:
- each frozen entry needs a one-bit cause;
- NewKey stays mandatory only for evidence freezes;
- nodes must still carry evidence against a self-frozen name, so a double-sign followed by a self-freeze in the same block cannot be released on the convicted key.
